{"type":"user","uuid":"01a0a96b-3fee-7fe0-8420-4c36f05c81ae","session_id":"01a0a96a-c480-78d1-9343-f2624a150c34","message":{"content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_NEqT9V96G07dPmu01Rc6hY7s","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"query\": \"TypeScript satisfies operator what does it do official documentation\",\n  \"totalResults\": 10,\n  \"showing\": 10,\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"url\": \"typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/release-notes/typescript-4-9.html\",\n      \"title\": \"Documentation - TypeScript 4.9\",\n      \"snippet\": \"TypeScript: Documentation - TypeScript 4.9\\n\\n## The `satisfies` Operator\\n\\nTypeScript developers are often faced with a dilemma: we want to ensure that some expression matches some type, but also want to keep the most specific type of that expression for inference purposes.\\n\\nFor example:\\n\\nts\\n\\n// Each property can be a string or an RGB tuple.\\n\\nconst palette = {\\n\\n red: [255, 0, 0],\\n\\n green: \\\"#00ff00\\\",\\n\\n bleu: [0, 0, 255]\\n\\n// ^^^^ sacrebleu - we've made a typo!\\n\\n};\\n\\n// We want to be able to use strin\",\n      \"source\": \"exa\",\n      \"extra\": {\n        \"trustLabel\": {\n          \"source\": \"retrieved\",\n          \"traceId\": \"66f22a537be94172bcef31ad5e5bdde1\"\n        },\n        \"trustDisposal\": \"accepted\",\n        \"trustSuspicious\": false\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"github.com/microsoft/TypeScript-Website/blob/v2/packages/documentation/copy/en/release-notes/TypeScript%204.9.md\",\n      \"title\": \"packages/documentation/copy/en/release-notes/TypeScript%204.9.md\",\n      \"snippet\": \"# packages/documentation/copy/en/release-notes/TypeScript%204.9.md\\n\\n- Branch: v2\\n- Repository: microsoft/TypeScript-Website\\n\\n---\\n\\n---\\ntitle: TypeScript 4.9\\nlayout: docs\\npermalink: /docs/handbook/release-notes/typescript-4-9.html\\noneline: TypeScript 4.9 Release Notes\\n---\\n\\n## The `satisfies` Operator\\n\\nTypeScript developers are often faced with a dilemma: we want to ensure that some expression *matches* some type, but also want to keep the *most specific* type of that expression for inference purpo\",\n      \"source\": \"exa\",\n      \"extra\": {\n        \"trustLabel\": {\n          \"source\": \"retrieved\",\n          \"traceId\": \"66f22a537be94172bcef31ad5e5bdde1\"\n        },\n        \"trustDisposal\": \"accepted\",\n        \"trustSuspicious\": false\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"github.com/Microsoft/TypeScript/commit/164dddc48e1eaa83780b8e72bc7afeebe768437b\",\n      \"title\": \"164dddc feat(7481): Operator to ensure an expression is contextually typed by, and satisfies, some type (#46827)\",\n      \"snippet\": \"# 164dddc feat(7481): Operator to ensure an expression is contextually typed by, and satisfies, some type (#46827)\\n\\n- SHA: 164dddc48e1eaa83780b8e72bc7afeebe768437b\\n- Repository: microsoft/TypeScript\\n- Author: a-tarasyuk\\n- Date: 2022-08-26T17:05:52Z\\n- +3172 -449 in 102 files\\n- Verified: yes\\n\\n---\\n\\nfeat(7481): Operator to ensure an expression is contextually typed by, and satisfies, some type (#46827)\\n\\n* feat(7481): add explicit type compatibility check with 'satisfies' expression \\n \\n* Add failing \",\n      \"source\": \"exa\",\n      \"extra\": {\n        \"trustLabel\": {\n          \"source\": \"retrieved\",\n          \"traceId\": \"66f22a537be94172bcef31ad5e5bdde1\"\n        },\n        \"trustDisposal\": \"accepted\",\n        \"trustSuspicious\": false\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"totaltypescript.com/clarifying-the-satisfies-operator\",\n      \"title\": \"Clarifying the `satisfies` Operator\",\n      \"snippet\": \"Clarifying the `satisfies` Operator | Total TypeScript\\n\\n# Clarifying the `satisfies` Operator\\n\\nMatt Pocock Matt is a well-regarded TypeScript expert known for his ability to demystify complex TypeScript concepts.\\n\\nTypeScript's satisfies operator has been out for a while, but it still seems to be a source of confusion that could use some clearing up.\\n\\nThink of satisfies as another way to assign types to values.\\n\\nBefore we look closer, let's do a review of how to assign types.\\n\\nFirst, there's the \",\n      \"source\": \"exa\",\n      \"extra\": {\n        \"trustLabel\": {\n          \"source\": \"retrieved\",\n          \"traceId\": \"66f22a537be94172bcef31ad5e5bdde1\"\n        },\n        \"trustDisposal\": \"accepted\",\n        \"trustSuspicious\": false\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"blog.logrocket.com/getting-started-typescript-satisfies-operator/\",\n      \"title\": \"Getting started with the TypeScript satisfies operator - LogRocket Blog\",\n      \"snippet\": \"Getting started with the TypeScript satisfies operator - LogRocket Blog\\n\\n May 5, 2023 ⋅ 5 min read \\n\\n# Getting started with the TypeScript `satisfies` operator\\n\\n Temitope Oyedele I am a web developer and technical writer. I love to write about things I've learned and experienced. \\n\\n Table of contents \\n\\n- What is the TypeScript `satisfies` operator?\\n\\n- Life before the `satisfies` operator\\n- Introducing the `satisfies` operator\\n\\n- Property name constraining\\n- Property name fulfillment\\n- Property v\",\n      \"source\": \"exa\",\n      \"publishedAt\": \"2023-05-05T00:00:00.000Z\",\n      \"extra\": {\n        \"trustLabel\": {\n          \"source\": \"retrieved\",\n          \"traceId\": \"66f22a537be94172bcef31ad5e5bdde1\"\n        },\n        \"trustDisposal\": \"accepted\",\n        \"trustSuspicious\": false\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"totaltypescript.com/how-to-use-satisfies-operator\",\n      \"title\": \"5 Ways to Use 'Satisfies' in TypeScript | Total TypeScript\",\n      \"snippet\": \"5 Ways to Use 'Satisfies' in TypeScript | Total TypeScript\\n\\n# 5 Ways to Use 'Satisfies' in TypeScript\\n\\nMatt Pocock Matt is a well-regarded TypeScript expert known for his ability to demystify complex TypeScript concepts.\\n\\nThe `satisfies` operator gives you a way to add type annotations to values without losing the inference of the value.\\n\\nIf you want to learn more, I've got a deep dive in this article.\\n\\nBut here, let's dive deep into some use cases.\\n\\n## Strongly typed URL Search Params with sati\",\n      \"source\": \"exa\",\n      \"publishedAt\": \"2023-08-02T00:00:00.000Z\",\n      \"extra\": {\n        \"trustLabel\": {\n          \"source\": \"retrieved\",\n          \"traceId\": \"66f22a537be94172bcef31ad5e5bdde1\"\n        },\n        \"trustDisposal\": \"accepted\",\n        \"trustSuspicious\": false\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"stackoverflow.com/questions/78636946/what-are-the-differences-between-typescript-s-satisfies-operator-and-type-assert\",\n      \"title\": \"What are the differences Between TypeScript's satisfies ...\",\n      \"snippet\": \"# What are the differences Between TypeScript’s satisfies operator and type assertions?\\n\\n- Tags: typescript\\n- Score: 33\\n- Views: 18,235\\n- Answers: 2\\n- Asked by: Roy Christo (819 rep)\\n- Asked on: Jun 18, 2024\\n- Last active: Oct 18, 2025\\n- License: CC BY-SA 4.0\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Question\\n\\nI've recently come across the `satisfies` operator in TypeScript and I'm trying to understand how it differs from regular type assertions. Both seem to provide a way to work with types, but I'm not clear on when to use on\",\n      \"source\": \"exa\",\n      \"publishedAt\": \"2024-09-16T08:52:44.186Z\",\n      \"extra\": {\n        \"trustLabel\": {\n          \"source\": \"retrieved\",\n          \"traceId\": \"66f22a537be94172bcef31ad5e5bdde1\"\n        },\n        \"trustDisposal\": \"accepted\",\n        \"trustSuspicious\": false\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"freecodecamp.org/news/typescript-satisfies-operator/\",\n      \"title\": \"How to Use the TypeScript satisfies Operator\",\n      \"snippet\": \"How to Use the TypeScript satisfies Operator\\n\\n July 10, 2023 / #TypeScript \\n\\n# How to Use the TypeScript satisfies Operator\\n\\nBy Satyam Tripathi\\n\\nIn TypeScript, the `satisfies` operator is a very useful tool. It was introduced in TypeScript v4.9 as an effective way to ensure type safety.\\n\\nThe `satisfies` operator tells you whether a given type satisfies a particular condition – and it provides this information before running your code. Also, when you're using it, you can declare a new variable to\",\n      \"source\": \"exa\",\n      \"publishedAt\": \"2023-07-10T00:00:00.000Z\",\n      \"extra\": {\n        \"trustLabel\": {\n          \"source\": \"retrieved\",\n          \"traceId\": \"66f22a537be94172bcef31ad5e5bdde1\"\n        },\n        \"trustDisposal\": \"accepted\",\n        \"trustSuspicious\": false\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"dataa.dev/2022/10/10/typescript-4-9-the-satisfies-operator-explained/\",\n      \"title\": \"TypeScript 4.9: The satisfies Operator Explained – C4: Container, Code, Cloud & Context\",\n      \"snippet\": \"TypeScript 4.9: The satisfies Operator Explained – C4: Container, Code, Cloud & Context\\n\\n# TypeScript 4.9: The satisfies Operator Explained\\n\\n Posted on October 10, 2022 by Nithin Mohan TK 3 min read \\n\\nTypeScript 4.9 introduces the `satisfies` operator—a new way to validate that expressions match a type without widening. This solves a long-standing tension between type inference and type checking. In this guide, I’ll explain when to use `satisfies` vs type annotations, common patterns, and migrat\",\n      \"source\": \"exa\",\n      \"publishedAt\": \"2022-10-10T09:00:00.000Z\",\n      \"extra\": {\n        \"trustLabel\": {\n          \"source\": \"retrieved\",\n          \"traceId\": \"66f22a537be94172bcef31ad5e5bdde1\"\n        },\n        \"trustDisposal\": \"accepted\",\n        \"trustSuspicious\": false\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"refine.dev/blog/typescript-satisfies-operator/\",\n      \"title\": \"How to Use the TypeScript satisfies Operator\",\n      \"snippet\": \"Skip to main content\\n\\nAll posts\\n\\nJAN 14, 202514 min read\\n\\n# How to Use the TypeScript satisfies Operator\\n\\nTutorials Abdullah Numan\\n\\nShare on:\\n\\nThis article was last updated on January 14, 2025, to include best practices for using the TypeScript satisfies operator, a detailed table comparing satisfies with type annotations and assertions, and tips for avoiding common mistakes when validating nested object types, using utility types like Partial, and working with complex type structures.\\n\\n## Intro\",\n      \"source\": \"exa\",\n      \"publishedAt\": \"2025-01-14T00:00:00.000Z\",\n      \"extra\": {\n        \"trustLabel\": {\n          \"source\": \"retrieved\",\n          \"traceId\": \"66f22a537be94172bcef31ad5e5bdde1\"\n        },\n        \"trustDisposal\": \"accepted\",\n        \"trustSuspicious\": false\n      }\n    }\n  ],\n  \"answers\": [\n    \"In TypeScript, the satisfies operator validates that the type of an expression is compatible with a target type without changing (replacing) the expression’s inferred type. This lets you get type-checked safety while keeping narrower/literal types for better inference and autocomplete [1][2]. Example: const palette = {...} satisfies Record<Colors, string | RGB>; catches incompatible values (and key issues) but palette keeps its more specific inferred property types [1][2].\"\n  ],\n  \"answersAvailable\": true,\n  \"providerAnswers\": [\n    {\n      \"provider\": \"exa\",\n      \"text\": \"In TypeScript, the satisfies operator validates that the type of an expression is compatible with a target type without changing (replacing) the expression’s inferred type. This lets you get type-checked safety while keeping narrower/literal types for better inference and autocomplete [1][2]. Example: const palette = {...} satisfies Record<Colors, string | RGB>; catches incompatible values (and key issues) but palette keeps its more specific inferred pr